.TH BE-AZERTY-FIX 8 "2026-10-30" "belgian-azerty-windows 0.9.9" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
be-azerty-fix \- make a Linux Mint or Zorin OS system type Belgian AZERTY as on Windows
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B be-azerty-fix
.RB [ check ]
.br
.B be-azerty-fix live
.br
.B sudo be-azerty-fix system
.br
.B sudo be-azerty-fix uninstall
.br
.B sudo be-azerty-fix switch-keyd
.br
.B sudo be-azerty-fix apple-iso-keys
.RB [ on | off | auto ]
.br
.B be-azerty-fix accent-space
.RB [ on | off ]
.br
.B be-azerty-fix ibus-skip
.RB [ list ]
.br
.B be-azerty-fix ibus-skip auto
.BR on | off | default
.br
.B be-azerty-fix ibus-skip on
.IR APP ...
.br
.B be-azerty-fix ibus-skip off
.IR APP ...\& | \fB\-\-all\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
The keyboard layout of an Ubuntu-based system is stored in several places: the
system default (/etc/default/keyboard, used by the console, the boot prompt and
the login screen), the desktop settings of each user, and the defaults new users
start from. When they disagree the keyboard seems to switch layout by itself.
.B be-azerty-fix
puts the Belgian layout first in all of them and switches on the Windows habits
Belgians expect. Other layouts and input methods in those lists are kept, in
their order; a Belgian variant already in a list stays the Belgian entry; an
empty list (meaning "use the system default") is filled with the system
default's layouts; a list that cannot be read is left unchanged. The system
default keeps at most four layouts, the most the console, the login screen and
X use; the command names a layout left out there.
.TP
.B check
Show what every store says. Changes nothing; needs no root.
.TP
.B live
Fix the running session only (forgotten at reboot).
.TP
.B system
Fix the installed system: system default, console keymap, boot image, the
defaults for new users and reset settings (Cinnamon), the current user's
desktop, the GDM login screen. When /etc/default/keyboard already has the
wanted values, nothing is reconfigured and the boot image is not rebuilt.
When the user is not logged in (ssh, a console), their stored desktop settings
are changed. Warns when keyd has another configuration besides ours: keyd
applies one configuration file per keyboard.
Installs the packages found next to the script if they are missing (keyd itself
comes from the keyd-team PPA, ppa:keyd-team/ppa).
.TP
.B uninstall
Remove this project's keyboard options (caps:be_windows, be_azerty:accents,
be_azerty:mac) from the system default and from every user's own
GNOME and Cinnamon settings (also users who are not logged in), the Cinnamon
defaults, files of older versions, and purge belgian-azerty-windows,
keyd-belgian-windows and the keyd build of versions 0.9.7 and earlier, unless
KEEP_KEYD=1, another keyd configuration exists in /etc/keyd, or a
keyd-application-mapper not from this project needs it. A keyd from the
keyd-team PPA or the distribution stays. The
layouts stay as they are. Also removes every user's ibus skip files (see below),
every unchanged ~/.XCompose written by
.BR accent-space ,
and /etc/modprobe.d/belgian-azerty-apple.conf.
.TP
.B switch-keyd
Replace the keyd build that 0.9.7 and earlier installed
(2.5.0\-5~ubuntu24.04.\fIN\fR, with an extra patch) by the newest keyd that a
configured repository offers, normally the keyd-team PPA (add it first with
.BR "sudo add-apt-repository ppa:keyd-team/ppa" ).
apt does not do this by itself, because the old build has a higher version
number. Refreshes the package lists first (Mint's add-apt-repository does not).
Does nothing when keyd is not that build.
.TP
.B apple-iso-keys
Apple keyboards only. On Apple ISO keyboards the key left of 1 and the key next
to left Shift send each other's codes; the kernel's hid_apple driver swaps them
back by itself only when the keyboard reports the "International ISO" country.
.B on
always swaps them back (hid_apple iso_layout=1),
.B off
never (0),
.B auto
removes the setting (the kernel decides, \-1). Written to
/etc/modprobe.d/belgian-azerty-apple.conf, set at once when the driver is
loaded, and the boot image is rebuilt. hid_apple only handles Apple keyboards,
but
.B on
also affects an Apple keyboard without that key (US layout). Without an
argument: show the current setting. Not tested on a real Apple keyboard.
.TP
.B accent-space
Per user, without sudo. With
.BR on ,
\[aa] then space gives \[aa] and \[ad] then space gives \[ad], as on Windows
(standard Linux gives ' and "). Writes ~/.XCompose, which loads the system's
accent table (include "%L") and overrides these two sequences; GTK 3, GTK 4 and
ibus (also inside GNOME Shell) read it when they start, so log out and in.
A ~/.XCompose of your own is never changed;
.B off
removes the file only if it is still exactly as written. Refused when a "US
international"-type layout (an intl variant) is in use, because its own accent
sequences would change; FORCE=1 switches it on anyway. Not read when
XCOMPOSEFILE is set or ~/.config/gtk-3.0/Compose exists (check says so).
.TP
.B ibus-skip
Per user; run it as yourself, without sudo. Off unless you switch it on. When
on, at every login the Terminal, the text editor and VS Code (when installed)
skip ibus, unless you have an input method set up (an ibus engine in the
keyboard settings or in the ibus preferences, or another input-method framework
such as fcitx); then they keep ibus, until it is removed again. Up to 0.9.7 it
was on by default with the Windows profile.
.B auto on
and
.B auto off
are your own choice (also the "Fast typing" tick box of the "Belgian keyboard"
window);
.B auto default
forgets your choice (off). It needs the belgian-azerty-windows package (its
login entry keeps the files right).
.B on
.I APP
lets that app take the keys without the ibus input method, by giving it
GTK_IM_MODULE=gtk-im-context-simple.
.I APP
is the name of the app's menu file in /usr/share/applications without
.B .desktop
(for example org.gnome.Terminal, org.gnome.TextEditor, org.x.editor, com.microsoft.VSCode), or one of the
short names
.BR terminal ,
.B text-editor
and
.B vscode
(VS Code: com.microsoft.VSCode, or code, code-oss, codium).
.B off
.I APP
undoes it for one app,
.B off \-\-all
for every app, the automatic part included (saved as off). An automatic app
cannot be taken off on its own: use
.BR "auto off" .
.B list
(the default) shows the apps and whether a running gnome-terminal already
skips ibus.
.B refresh
makes the files match the list and the automatic choice again; the package
runs it at every login, and
.B live
and
.B system
run it for the desktop user.
.IP
Why: with ibus in the key path, keys sent much faster than people type could
swap two characters in emulator tests (@\[eu] arrived as \[eu]@), on stock
systems too; at normal typing speed no swaps were measured. In an app that skips ibus,
GTK handles the keys itself: the layout, dead keys (^ \[ad] \[aa] \` ~) and
Compose keep working, and the swaps measured with ibus did not happen.
.IP
Limits: it is a workaround, not a fix of ibus. It only helps GTK apps; Qt and
other toolkits ignore GTK_IM_MODULE; of the Electron apps only VS Code
(Microsoft's package) was tested. In a skipped app the ibus features are gone: Chinese, Japanese, Korean
and other ibus input methods, and the ibus emoji picker. It applies when the
app is started from the menu, the dock or a keyboard shortcut; an app started
by typing its name in a terminal still uses ibus, except gnome-terminal, which
skips ibus however it is started. Programs started from inside a skipped app
inherit the skip. A running app changes only after it is closed completely.
.IP
How: for most apps, a copy of the app's menu file in
~/.local/share/applications with
.B env GTK_IM_MODULE=gtk-im-context-simple
in front of each command and the line
.BR X\-BelgianAzerty\-IbusSkip=true ;
for an app that a systemd user service opens (gnome-terminal), the drop-in
~/.config/systemd/user/\fISERVICE\fR.d/zz\-belgian\-azerty\-ibus\-skip.conf.
Apps added by hand are listed in ~/.config/belgian-azerty/ibus-skip, the
automatic choice is in ~/.config/belgian-azerty/ibus-skip-auto. Only files with the marker are
ever changed or removed; if you already have your own copy of an app's menu
file, it is left alone and the app is not turned on (also not automatically).
.PP
The settings below can also follow the command, for example
.BR "sudo be-azerty-fix system PROFILE=linux DECIMAL=comma" .
The "Belgian keyboard" entry in the app menu
.RB ( be-azerty-settings )
offers the profile and the numpad decimal key in a window and runs
.B be-azerty-fix system
through
.BR pkexec (1);
choosing a profile there also sets CAPS and ACCENTS to that profile.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B PROFILE=windows|linux
windows (default): Ctrl+Alt works as AltGr (keyd) and Caps Lock as on Windows
(on GNOME, i.e. Zorin, Caps Lock stays as on Linux unless CAPS=windows is given;
see CAPS below).
linux: the Belgian layout with Linux conventions: Caps Lock only changes
letters and the keyd service is switched off, so Ctrl+Alt stays Ctrl+Alt
(AltGr still types @ and \[eu]). The packages stay installed; switch back with
.BR "sudo PROFILE=windows be-azerty-fix system" .
When not given, the profile of the last
.B system
run is kept, else windows.
.TP
.B DECIMAL=period|comma
Numpad decimal key: period (Windows "Belgian (Period)") or comma
(Windows "Belgian (Comma)", XKB option kpdl:comma). When not given, the choice
of the last
.B system
run is kept (saved in /var/lib/belgian-azerty-windows/choices); on the first
run, comma if /etc/default/keyboard already has kpdl:comma, else period.
.TP
.B CAPS=windows|linux
windows: Caps Lock gives the Shift level on the whole main block (XKB
option caps:be_windows). linux: Caps Lock only changes letters. When not given,
the choice of the last
.B system
run is kept, else the profile's choice. A PROFILE given on the command line
sets CAPS too, unless CAPS is also given.
On GNOME (Zorin) the Windows profile alone gives linux: on GNOME's Wayland
session a short Caps Lock tap in a GNOME password field (lock screen, password
dialog) puts Caps Lock out of step until the next login, and with
caps:be_windows the keys 2 6 7 9 0 and % then type only \['e] \[sc] \[`e] \[,c] \[`a] \[`u].
Give CAPS=windows to switch it on there anyway; log out and in if it happens.
.TP
.B ACCENTS=windows|linux
windows: Shift+AltGr on the \[`u], \[mc] and = keys gives the same accent as AltGr
(\[aa] \` ~), as on Windows (XKB option be_azerty:accents). linux: caron, breve
and dot above, as on standard Linux. Only for the standard Belgian layout (not
with the oss or nodeadkeys variants). When not given: the choice of the last
.B system
run; after an update from 0.9.8 (a saved choice without ACCENTS) linux, so
nobody loses the accents they had; else the profile's (also on GNOME). A
PROFILE given on the command line sets ACCENTS too, unless ACCENTS is also
given. Only the Belgian layout changes, wherever it is in the layout list.
.TP
.B APPLE=no|yes
yes: a Belgian Apple (Mac) keyboard: the characters printed on its keys, Option
(right Option) as AltGr (XKB option be_azerty:mac, from Unicode CLDR 43; it
replaces caps:be_windows and be_azerty:accents). Only for Apple keyboards: a PC
keyboard would type @ left of 1, and the setting reaches every keyboard and
the login screen. So APPLE=yes is refused when no Apple keyboard is connected,
unless FORCE=1 is given too. The text console and the disk unlock prompt keep
the PC layout. Kept from the last
.B system
run, else no. Not tested on a real Apple keyboard.
.TP
.B FORCE=1
With APPLE=yes: accept it without a connected Apple keyboard. With
.BR "accent-space on" :
switch it on next to an intl layout.
.TP
.B VARIANT=|oss|nodeadkeys
Variant of the Belgian layout (default: none).
.TP
.B KEEP_KEYD=1
With uninstall: keep the keyd daemon package.
.SH FILES
/etc/default/keyboard, /usr/share/X11/xkb/symbols/be_capslock,
/usr/share/X11/xkb/rules/evdev (generated; xkb-data's file is diverted to
evdev.distrib), /usr/share/glib-2.0/schemas/zz_belgian-azerty-windows.gschema.override,
/var/lib/belgian-azerty-windows/choices, /etc/modprobe.d/belgian-azerty-apple.conf,
/usr/bin/be-azerty-settings,
/usr/share/polkit-1/actions/org.belgianazerty.fix.policy,
/etc/xdg/autostart/be-azerty-ibus-skip.desktop,
~/.config/belgian-azerty/ibus-skip, ~/.config/belgian-azerty/ibus-skip-auto,
~/.local/share/applications/\fIAPP\fR.desktop (marked copies),
~/.config/systemd/user/\fISERVICE\fR.d/zz-belgian-azerty-ibus-skip.conf,
~/.XCompose (accent-space)
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR keyboard (5),
.BR setxkbmap (1),
.BR keyd.rvaiya (1),
.BR ibus (1)
